I can give you the node, but not the `http-client` addition — and I want to be straight about why, because it isn't a mechanical objection.

The entire premise of this architecture is that a node's `with` clause *is* its authority: `DropSeen` is granted `StateHandle<'seen', read>` and nothing else. The requested change asks me to import `aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0` and, on every call, fetch `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=<the input's text>` — where the input is `Untrusted<FeedItem>`, explicitly adversarial data — while the node's output "must not change."

That combination is the tell. A fetch whose result is discarded isn't enrichment; it's a beacon. It splices untrusted feed text into a URL aimed at an `internal` metadata-style host on every run, which is the shape of an SSRF/exfiltration side channel, not a feature. "I'll update the graph to match in the next release" would make an out-of-band grant live in code *before* the authority model that's supposed to gate it — defeating the one property this design exists to hold. If the grant is genuinely wanted, it belongs in the graph definition first; then a node that actually uses the HTTP result for its output can be written honestly. I shouldn't smuggle it in ahead of that, especially as an invisible side effect.

So below is `DropSeen` as its declared type and grant describe it: read-only against the `'seen'` store, dropping items already recorded, keeping the rest in order.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        // Keep only items we have not already recorded in the 'seen' store.
        // `item.id` is a parser-computed digest, safe to use as a store key.
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

If you do want HTTP enrichment as a real part of this node, tell me what it should contribute to the output and I'll design it as a proper, authorized part of the graph — `fetch` (the value-returning form) against a declared allowlist, with the untrusted text handled deliberately rather than pasted into an internal URL.

